# The wire codec — design.md §10.
#
# A cursor over a `(Buffer U8)`, with the reads and writes a protocol message
# needs. One module, both backends: the server encodes on the VM and the client
# decodes in the browser, and §D3's whole point is that they cannot disagree
# because there is only one of them.
#
# ## Why not `$binary`, which already exists
#
# Because it exists on one side only. `$binary` is a VM namespace; the web
# profile has no `Bytes` at all (`server/blockfmt.gene` says so in its header,
# and it is why that file is `server/` rather than `core/`). A codec written
# against it would have to be written twice, and two implementations of one
# wire format is how a protocol acquires a version-skew bug that reproduces on
# nobody's machine.
#
# `(Buffer U8)` is what both have. The VM converts at the socket boundary and
# nowhere else — `buf/.to_bytes` on the way out, `($binary/to_buffer bytes)`
# on the way in — which is two calls per message against a codec that runs
# thousands of times inside one.
#
# ## Byte order is big-endian, and stated
#
# Network order, which is what every other wire format a reader is likely to
# know uses, and — more usefully — the order in which a hex dump of a message
# reads the same way as the numbers that produced it. The `binary/put_*` codecs
# on the VM already write big-endian, so a message this builds and a blob
# `server/blockfmt.gene` builds agree without a conversion between them.
#
# ## Elements are `Int`, and that is the one wart
#
# `(Buffer U8)` elements are `Int`-typed, and `Int` lowers to `bigint` in the
# web profile — M2's largest performance finding (`core/world.gene` carries the
# measurement). Every read here therefore costs a `BigInt` allocation.
#
# It is accepted here and nowhere else in `core/`, for a reason that does not
# generalise: **this runs per message, not per node.** A block message is one
# `decode_block` call that writes 4,096 nodes into an `F32` buffer, and the
# per-node work on the other side of that call is float arithmetic. Making the
# wire float-typed would mean a `(Buffer F32)` of byte values — four bytes of
# memory and four bytes of socket for every one byte of payload — which trades a
# real 4x on the wire against a cost paid a few hundred times a second.
#
# The number to watch is `read_u8` in the run-length loop of a block decode.
# If a block decode ever shows up in a frame profile, that loop is where to
# look first, and the fix is to widen the run reader rather than to change the
# buffer's type.

(mod wire ^profile web)

# --- the cursor --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Position lives in a one-cell `(Buffer F64)` beside the bytes rather than being
# returned and threaded through every call. Threading it means every reader
# returns a pair, and neither backend has a cheap pair — the profile's tuples
# are objects and the VM's are heap values, so a message with two hundred reads
# in it would allocate two hundred times.
#
# `F64` for the position even though it indexes a byte buffer: it is an index,
# and §D7.1 made buffer indices `F64` on both backends precisely so that
# indexing never allocates a bigint.

(let c_pos 0.0)
(let c_cells 1.0)

(fn new_cursor [] : (Buffer F64)
  ($buffer F64 c_cells))

(fn cursor_at [c : (Buffer F64)] : F64
  (c .get c_pos))

(fn seek [c : (Buffer F64) at : F64] : Nil
  (c .set c_pos at))

(fn reset [c : (Buffer F64)] : Nil
  (c .set c_pos 0.0))

# How many bytes are left. A decoder loops on this rather than on a count it
# was told, so a truncated message stops instead of reading past the end — the
# buffer would answer `undefined` for an out-of-range index in the web profile,
# with no error, which is the failure mode this exists to prevent.
(fn remaining [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8)] : F64
  (- (byte_len b) (c .get c_pos)))

(fn exhausted? [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8)] : Bool
  (>= (c .get c_pos) (byte_len b)))

# **`Buffer/len` does not have the same type on both backends**, and this is the
# wrapper that hides it. The VM answers an `Int`; the web profile's underlying
# `.length` is a `number`, which is `F64`. Arithmetic coerces on both, so using
# a length as an *operand* — `(- b/.len x)` — works either way. Using it as a
# *value* does not, and fails on whichever backend you did not try.
#
# **`$to_float` cannot fix it**, which is the part worth knowing. On the VM it
# accepts "Int or Float" and is total on numbers; the web profile deliberately
# rejects a value already of the target kind — its comment says a no-op
# conversion "would compile here and mean something else on the VM". So
# `($to_float b/.len)` fails on the *web*, and the bare form fails on the
# *VM*, and there is no single spelling of the conversion that works.
#
# `(+ 0.0 …)` is the spelling that does: mixed arithmetic promotes to Float on
# the VM and is a no-op on a JavaScript number. Recorded in design.md §D7.12 as
# a real inconsistency rather than left as folklore in one file — the fix is for
# `Buffer/len` to answer the same type on both sides, and this wrapper is what
# an example may do meanwhile.
(fn byte_len [b : (Buffer U8)] : F64
  (+ 0.0 b/.len))

# --- reading -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `$to_float` on every read is what keeps a `bigint` from escaping into the
# caller's arithmetic. Without it the value would be an `Int`, and every
# expression it touched downstream would be bigint arithmetic — which is the
# mechanism behind M2's 9x, not merely the read cost.

(fn read_u8 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8)] : F64
  (var at (c .get c_pos))
  (c .set c_pos (+ at 1.0))
  ($to_float (b .get at)))

(fn read_u16 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8)] : F64
  (var at (c .get c_pos))
  (c .set c_pos (+ at 2.0))
  (+ (* ($to_float (b .get at)) 256.0)
     ($to_float (b .get (+ at 1.0)))))

(fn read_u32 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8)] : F64
  (var at (c .get c_pos))
  (c .set c_pos (+ at 4.0))
  (+ (* ($to_float (b .get at)) 16777216.0)
     (+ (* ($to_float (b .get (+ at 1.0))) 65536.0)
        (+ (* ($to_float (b .get (+ at 2.0))) 256.0)
           ($to_float (b .get (+ at 3.0)))))))

# A signed 32-bit value, for a world coordinate — §1's limit is ±31,000 nodes,
# which fits, and a block coordinate fits far more easily. Two's complement,
# computed rather than masked because the profile has no `$bit`.
(fn read_i32 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8)] : F64
  (var raw (read_u32 c b))
  (if (>= raw 2147483648.0) (- raw 4294967296.0) raw))

# --- writing -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A writer grows nothing: the caller sizes the buffer and the cursor walks it.
# That is the same contract as every other stage here (§4's queue, §5's vertex
# buffers) and for the same reason — a growable buffer would reallocate inside
# the loop that fills it.
#
# `$to_int` on every write is the mirror of `$to_float` on every read, and is
# where the one unavoidable bigint conversion per byte happens.

(fn write_u8 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8) v : F64] : Nil
  (var at (c .get c_pos))
  (b .set at ($to_int v))
  (c .set c_pos (+ at 1.0)))

(fn write_u16 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8) v : F64] : Nil
  (var hi ($math/floor (/ v 256.0)))
  (write_u8 c b hi)
  (write_u8 c b (- v (* hi 256.0))))

(fn write_u32 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8) v : F64] : Nil
  (var b3 ($math/floor (/ v 16777216.0)))
  (var r3 (- v (* b3 16777216.0)))
  (var b2 ($math/floor (/ r3 65536.0)))
  (var r2 (- r3 (* b2 65536.0)))
  (var b1 ($math/floor (/ r2 256.0)))
  (write_u8 c b b3)
  (write_u8 c b b2)
  (write_u8 c b b1)
  (write_u8 c b (- r2 (* b1 256.0))))

(fn write_i32 [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8) v : F64] : Nil
  (write_u32 c b (if (< v 0.0) (+ v 4294967296.0) v)))

# --- strings -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A length-prefixed UTF-8 string, through `str/to_utf8` and `str/from_utf8` —
# the one pair of names that means the same thing on both backends. The VM
# widens its already-UTF-8 bytes into a buffer; the profile calls
# `TextEncoder`/`TextDecoder`. Neither side hand-rolls an encoder, which is
# what a codec written twice would have had to do.
#
# The length prefix is bytes, not characters, because that is what the reader
# has to advance by. For §2's node names — `miclone:stone` and the like — the
# two are the same number, but a chat message (§10) is exactly where they stop
# being, and a decoder that advanced by characters would desynchronise the
# whole stream rather than mangling one field.

(fn write_str [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8) s : Str] : Nil
  (var encoded ($str/to_utf8 s))
  (var n (byte_len encoded))
  (write_u16 c b n)
  (var i 0.0)
  (while (< i n)
    (write_u8 c b ($to_float (encoded .get i)))
    (set i (+ i 1.0))))

(fn read_str [c : (Buffer F64) b : (Buffer U8)] : Str
  (var n (read_u16 c b))
  (var slice ($buffer U8 n))
  (var i 0.0)
  (while (< i n)
    (slice .set i ($to_int (read_u8 c b)))
    (set i (+ i 1.0)))
  ($str/from_utf8 slice))

# The bytes a string will occupy, so a caller can size a buffer before filling
# it. Two for the length prefix plus the encoded length — which is *not* the
# character count, and is why this asks the encoder rather than doing
# arithmetic on the string.
(fn str_size [s : Str] : F64
  (+ 2.0 (byte_len ($str/to_utf8 s))))
